WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.220
You are lost in a dark, perilous, and enchanted forest.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:11.420
Dark, dangerous mushrooms push up through the squishy forest floor, snaring you on every side.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.700
Something slimy flashes through the mushrooms, moving in on you.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.340
The millipede!

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.140
Not a normal-sized one, but the gigantic monster is attacking you.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.300
All you have to fight back with is the magic arrow.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.600
Shoot his head to turn him into mushrooms.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.820
But then, it is not the only thing attacking you.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.140
There are more creepy, crawly, buzzing, swarming creatures in this forest.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.340
Keep fighting with your magic arrow.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.440
If you are lucky, you may survive.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.740
Watch out! Here they come!

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.280
And welcome to NEStalgia!

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.960
Chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.100
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:18.920
And guys, I hope you're ready for a little arcade fever, because just about since September of 1988 through the rest of this year, we're just going to be playing a ton of arcade games.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.140
Well, I honestly thought that we were starting, like, the reading of an audiobook right there.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.020
Is there any more unique prose regarding Millipede?

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:36.500
Yeah, I just, and you know, other boxes have had very long things like that, but they never felt worthy of reading, but I think this one really set the mood.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.040
Yeah, I think that this guy, this guy should be doing more than backs of boxes.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.740
He should be doing backs of novels.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.100
I kind of want, like, a Millipede-Silent Hills crossover.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.860
Is that the, like, natural progression?

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.020
Like, backs of boxes, and then, like, you move up to backs of novels?

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.040
Yeah, yeah, it's back of, yeah, back of VHS's is somewhere in there, too, but...

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.920
Some people collect VHS boxes just because of that, they like the way those things look.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:13.260
Anyway, arcade fever, Capcom and Konami constantly releasing arcade hits all the time, but relatively quickly, we're getting, like, a lot of stuff.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:29.780
Like, from September and October of 1988, Namco has given us both Galaga and Xevious, Nintendo re-released the Donkey Kong classics, and now Hal Laboratories is joining with, well, they did join us with Joust, and now they're joining us again with Millipede here.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:36.500
So, anything to say about, like, older 1980s arcade games coming in this late into the decade?

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:47.100
I'm sure it was appreciated by the, just in the same way that, like, I love when an old classic game that I loved from PS1 is now available on my PS4.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:53.880
But I think to us, now looking back, that little level of appreciation might be lost, at least to me personally.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:00.300
Yeah, I kind of thought we were past this, but I don't think it's ever truly going to stop.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.080
It's, I think we're going to keep getting these spurts.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.580
Yeah, I mean, to be fair, they're, like, still releasing Millipede on probably Switch and PS4, right?

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.360
Like, they're just never going to stop releasing these games.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:21.040
Yeah, probably. And I wanted to talk about Hal Laboratories for a minute, because we did not cover them in the Joust episode, and I think it's kind of important that we talk about them.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.880
Like, they are pretty active today with some stuff.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.660
I just can't put my finger on what franchise they're attached to.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:34.280
Well, it's good that you recognize them, at least, because they're best known for Kirby and the creation of Kirby.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.700
Ah, yes. Mother, the Mother or Earthbound franchise, and Super Smash Brothers.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.460
I think that's what I recognize them from.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:43.400
That's where I see that little doggy.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.620
Yeah, there was that little thing on the menu screen, right?

00:03:46.540 --> 00:04:01.180
Yep. It's pretty much a Nintendo second party at this point, but back then, the only thing Hal Laboratory had as, like, a, you know, bargaining chip for Nintendo was they had Satoru Iwata as, like, one of their employees, and Nintendo loved that guy.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.360
He's such a nice guy.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.960
You know, the company actually got its name, Hal Laboratories.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:13.720
They got it because each letter of the H-A-L puts them one letter ahead of IBM.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.860
I think you're mixing that up with where Hal from 9000 got his name.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.400
Yeah, I was going to say, I thought maybe it was from 2001.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:22.520
I'm almost positive.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:33.030
Because I was going to point out that it's not, you know, another coincidence then is that IBM happened to release around, I want to say, like, early 2000s.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:41.650
2000s, they released IBM Millipede Memory, which was special memory that they had created for their processor chips.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:48.990
And I don't think it's unlikely that that would be to retaliate for Hal Lab releasing Millipede on the NES.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.350
And then IBM says, well, we can use the Millipede name, too.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:56.010
And even though you're three letters ahead of us, we're still better than you.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:59.410
It's funny, though, because there's, like, kind of two ways of looking at it.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.410
Like, that A is better than B because, like, you know, like, number one is better than number two.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.610
But also, you can be like, well, the later letters could be better.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:14.230
You know what I mean? Like, what determines that the earlier letter is, like, better than B?

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.050
I mean, the phone book.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.850
It's the phone book. Mike, I think Mike is just projecting all of this.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:23.670
You know, I think Mike has a thing about the, has a thing about the alphabet.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.790
And he's got a bone to pick with the letters I, B, and M.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:34.250
No, I actually, I just wanted to make the crazy connection there of IBM having, like, Millipede memory.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.490
And Hal Laboratory is making Millipede.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.550
And Hal Laboratory is basically just stealing the name from 2001 of Space Odyssey's Hal 9000.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.790
Which Iwata actually said in an interview.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.250
It was just a straight rip from Space Odyssey.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.390
So I think that's, I think that's all pretty funny.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:50.730
I just wanted to throw it out there.

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:52.070
It's a fun little menage a trois.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:53.950
Everything's in jest around here, okay?

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.550
We don't actually provide any real knowledge to anyone.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:08.150
If you want some real knowledge, Nintendo, the only reason why Hal Laboratories is still around today is because Nintendo made them a second party when they saved Hal from bankruptcy.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:13.470
On the condition that Hal employee Satoru Iwata was appointed its president.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:18.210
Wow. That's some, like, indentured servitude right there.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:19.530
So, so they did it.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.330
They said, yeah, you could be the president.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.610
And he was the president from 1993 to 2000.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:30.630
And then later became president of Nintendo until his early passing.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:35.430
So I think it's, like, it's pretty crazy how much they really didn't care for Hal Laboratories.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.770
And they just cared about this one programmer who was really good at making video games.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.390
And, you know, this is all to say with this Arcade Fever stuff.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.890
I mean, next week we've got Pac-Man from Tengen.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.830
So, like, things are constantly going to keep happening with arcades.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:49.910
I think that's enough about that.

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:50.970
And let's move on to Millipede.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.590
What do you guys say? Sounds good.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:54.270
I'm okay with that. All right.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:07:00.270
So, Sean, walk me through Millipede in a way that isn't as artsy as the back of the box.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:06.290
All right. So in Millipede, you play as a cursor at the bottom of the screen.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:15.830
And there is a very long centipede slash millipede slash creepy crawly quickly coming down towards you.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.910
And there's a bunch of, you know, foliage in the way.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:20.310
I think they're all mushrooms.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:21.230
I can't remember.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.670
And every time the millipede hits one, he changes direction.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.170
He's got to kill him.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:31.870
And every time you kill him, he breaks apart into little pieces.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.610
And you have to kill all those.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:34.550
And there are spiders.

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.350
And there are other bugs.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.330
And it's a little overwhelming.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.110
And that's pretty much how you play the game.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.750
Yeah. This is a lot like Space Invaders on speed, right?

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:49.590
Yeah. The Space Invader enemies, which might just be called invaders,

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.910
they move across the screen very, very slowly.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:55.210
And you have these barriers to protect you.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.990
And you kind of have to shoot all of them.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:08:00.670
But if they get to the bottom, then you're automatically game over.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:06.470
And Millipede, the millipede or, you know, the centipede, they both look the same to me.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.190
They come down the screen rapidly.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.810
But if they touch the bottom, that doesn't necessarily mean game over.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:16.210
You can dodge around them and continue to try to attack them.

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:21.650
So I think this is a great step up from Space Invaders in that regard.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:27.890
Like, how do we improve on an already existing, you know, successful arcade game?

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.250
And I think Millipede and centipede were the answer to that.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:35.510
Yeah. There's like a little end zone, if you will, that you're allowed to go forward and backward in.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.070
So you can't fly forward the whole way.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:42.250
But it was nice to be able to move forward a little bit to get around certain obstacles.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:55.950
Yeah, it's definitely necessary because the whole time that you're trying to hit this millipede, you're being assaulted by these spiders and like ladybugs and shit.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:04.350
And I think that it definitely makes it a higher octane experience than Space Invaders.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:14.570
Yeah. And so let's talk about just the, you know, the gameplay in terms of everything that the ratio of what's on screen and how much you have to deal with and stuff like that.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:18.210
Because it seems like it could be fun at first, but maybe it becomes overwhelming.

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:23.070
Joe, how did you feel about controlling your, I don't know, I want to call them like a little ship.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.430
Sean called them a cursor, which I think feels fine too.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:29.970
How did you feel about controlling that and being able to tackle everything that was on screen?

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:34.210
Well, as far as actually controlling the cursor, first of all, I don't know what that is.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.070
Is that supposed to be a person or like, are you, you're in the forest, right?

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:44.650
It looks like a ship to me though, but either way, controlling you yourself is pretty like dead on.

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:48.070
You know, there's no floatiness or not even like good floatiness that we have.

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.030
It's like literally you stop on a dime.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.130
It's pretty easy to actually control yourself.

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:57.790
But as far as the obstacles in front of you, you know, you start with a lot of these mushrooms that are in the way.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:06.950
That are kind of like little obstacles for you to kind of shoot past or shoot around or shoot through to hit the millipede.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:14.170
And one thing I think we haven't mentioned yet is that every time you hit a segment of the millipede, it turns into another mushroom.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:22.090
So like as you're doing better, you're creating more obstacles for yourself, which I thought was an interesting way to kind of keep things from getting too easy.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.650
And you're creating more millipedes too, Joe.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.890
When you shoot in the middle of a millipede, you divide it in half.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:32.730
Right. So it's still the same amount of targets, but now it's, now it's harder because they've, they've split up from each other.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:44.050
Mm-hmm. Uh, so yeah, they, they do a pretty good job of, of making sure that like the gameplay just organically continues to get harder without having to like, this is where we'll program.

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:48.330
I mean, not that they didn't do this, but they didn't have to do, uh, right away.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.450
Like, all right, now we're going to program it to be like more difficult.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:54.510
It'll just naturally get more difficult by the way of the way everything works.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:11:04.650
I think they do a little bit of both there because yeah, there's the organic difficulty increase by shooting the millipede, splitting it in half, creating more targets.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:14.830
But, uh, if you survive for what the game feels is too long, it will just start throwing more stuff at you until you die.

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:16.210
And then it sort of clears the screen.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:21.890
But in addition to like the spiders, you'll get like little gnats or flies, I don't know.

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:26.190
And they just sort of rain down on you, uh, if you're alive for too long.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:34.510
Um, and, uh, also there are other bugs along the way that have effects on the game that aren't as direct.

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:41.970
Like, I think depending on the color of the ladybug sort of looking thing, it'll shift the screen down or shift the screen up.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:43.210
I thought that was a snail.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.170
Hmm. Like maybe it's the snail.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.430
I don't remember it being associated with the snail.

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.210
Got it. But no, I mean, like, I think, I think it's a trade-off.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:57.550
Like, it's really cool that there's all this different stuff, you know, available to you to shoot at in the game.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:02.830
Like that they took a wide variety of insects and didn't just make them all like, hey, this is the enemies.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.270
Shoot them, get points.

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:08.350
It's like, oh, if you shoot the spiders, yeah, they're worth more points, which help you get more lives.

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:12.390
Or, oh, if you shoot the ladybug snail thing, everything moves down a bit.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:19.110
Or if you shoot those flies that come out in rapid succession and, you know, it's like a bonus stage style thing to rack up points quickly.

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.570
Like, I thought that stuff was cool, but it just, it happens all in unison.

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:27.010
So it's almost like a visual assault.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.930
I couldn't find myself focusing.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:31.910
No, it's, you're very right there.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:42.290
Because I think, like, when we talk about quiet time in games, we're usually talking about, like, you know, in an action adventure title where there aren't any enemies and you just sort of get to, like, look around.

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:53.110
But I think quiet time exists in arcades as well in a more small package where it's just, like, you have a moment to breathe.

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:57.150
And in this game, you never have a moment to breathe unless you died.

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:58.750
You have, like, two seconds.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.430
Yeah, there's no stage clear, right?

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:05.230
There's no moment where it resets everything for you.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:06.570
Am I wrong in thinking that?

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:08.770
It'll partially reset.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:17.710
Like, if you destroy every segment of the millipede, then the millipede goes back to the top and maybe some enemies despawn.

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:19.590
No, totally. But I meant, like, there's no...

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:28.770
Spiders don't. Yeah, but there's no moment of, like, stage clear up there and you have, like, a moment to collect yourself and the whole screen, like, refreshes it.

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:31.270
Yeah, exactly. It's just on death.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:40.810
Joe, you were talking about the movement, and I wanted to get your feeling on this because, obviously, the NES didn't have a trackball first-party controller.

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:42.390
I'm sure somebody made it third-party.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:49.090
But in the arcade, this game was only played with a trackball, so this way you could really get free roam movement on your cursor.

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:54.850
Do you think that that would make a huge difference in how you played, or you thought that playing on an NES controller was fine?

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:56.110
Well, only now that you mention it.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:14:08.550
I mean, I wasn't aware of that, so on the NES controller, it felt fine because it's all I know, and it felt like it was perfectly functional to me, especially compared to some other games that we've played in the past.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:09.810
I thought this was...

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:15.890
It didn't, like, blow me away with some impressive, you know, level of control, but it was just, like, perfectly, like, accurate.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:26.370
But I can see how it would completely change the way that you approach the game with a trackball, because you just have a different level of, you know, freedom, I guess?

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:30.490
Yeah. No, I would very much prefer to play this game with a trackball.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:35.830
Yeah. Yeah, now that, like, I know that that's out there, that does sound like it could be easier.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.330
But I don't think that that makes this one a bad way of controlling it.

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:41.950
It's just not as good.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:50.090
Yeah, and there also, there was a single fire button on the arcade version that could be held down for rapid fire.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.610
Now, I don't think you can do that with the NES controller, correct?

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:55.050
I think if you hold down A, it doesn't continue to shoot.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:04.930
Well, in addition to that, it only has the memory, or maybe not the memory, but it only allows one bullet to be on screen at a time.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:12.510
So, like, we've seen in several other games that if you miss and it hits the top of the screen, that's all time that you are just defenseless.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:15.750
I don't know if that's the way that it is in the arcade game.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.610
Do you have any information on that, Mike?

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:19.510
Right. No, that's true.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:25.570
You know, and I just tested it, too, and it does seem like it's probably the same.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:30.870
What they're describing as rapid fire is probably the same as it appears on the NES version.

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:38.170
That you can just hold it down and it'll continue to shoot, but you're only allowed one shoot per screen at a time.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:48.490
I see. You know, another thing that we didn't talk about that kind of affects the gameplay but really has nothing to do with it is the user interface here during the game.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:59.870
Because there's a large chunk of screen real estate dedicated to this brick background that just kind of houses in a mortise the gameplay itself.

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:05.910
And then there's, like, sections for your score and the top score and how many ships you have and stuff like that.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:12.410
But did it feel like that was just hogging too much of the screen for something that wasn't really using that much graphical power to begin with?

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.630
Absolutely. That's the first thing that I noticed.

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:21.490
I'm glad you mentioned that because it's, you know, on the right side, you have, like, a little bit.

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:25.650
You have your score. You've got, you know, like, what round you're on.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:29.530
At the bottom, you have, like, one little sentence that tells you, like, when your next bonus is.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:35.450
And then, but then, like, most of this frame around your gameplay is just complete empty space.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:45.790
So I'm not sure why, if there's a reason it had to be like this where, you know, some reason where it would have looked terrible because they didn't feel like updating, like, the pixel count to everything.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:47.810
So they wanted to keep it smaller or something.

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:50.930
I don't know. But it felt weird that we were playing it such a tiny.

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:53.450
It's almost like half the size of the regular screen.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:54.450
Maybe a little more than that.

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:59.430
But yeah, I'd say it's like 50, 60 percent of the screen space.

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:02.050
I actually didn't really pay any mind to it.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:04.110
But so it didn't bother me.

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:10.850
But now that I'm looking at it with a more critical eye, it is very weird because it's not even, like, justified correctly.

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:18.850
Yeah. We usually see things on the NES housed in mortises for I'm thinking, like, the goonies.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.130
Mortis. A mortis?

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:21.130
A mortis. Yeah.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:22.550
Yep. Interesting.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:28.290
We usually see that for, like, the goonies, you know, when you went into, like, the first person point of view.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:35.310
And that, I guess, had something to do with, like, you know, to convey a lot of information in a smaller screen or something like that.

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.130
And that makes sense there.

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.490
But here, you're not really getting a graphic update.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:43.630
You're actually getting a graphic downgrade from Millipede on the arcade.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:55.010
Yeah. And if you're not just looking at it, if you were to just take at the bottom where it says next bonus, put that on the side under everything else, you'd fill in some of the extra space that you're leaving on the right side.

00:17:55.050 --> 00:18:00.470
And then you can just remove the brick background from the bottom, the left, and the top.

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:05.650
And then you'd have just, you could just have one bar on the side of the screen if you really need that bar.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.470
I mean, granted, that would screw with the ratio and everything.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.750
But at that point...

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:25.530
Yeah, when you first mentioned it, like, again, because I didn't quite remember its, like, the dimensions, I thought it was to make it the same aspect ratio of a cabinet, which, you know, usually has, like, more of a vertical orientation.

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:27.110
But no, this is just a square.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:34.990
And I'm almost positive that it is at a lower resolution than in any other version of this game.

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:38.070
So, yeah, I don't know what their thoughts were.

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:42.230
Yeah, well, I think I also agree with you, Sean, though, that it wasn't distracting either.

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.110
So you can still play the game just fine.

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:45.750
It just feels almost weird, you know?

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:50.490
It doesn't harpen or do anything to the actual gameplay experience.

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:53.250
I don't think it would be any harder if it was bigger either, you know?

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.850
Yeah, no, it's just, I mean, all the information is there.

00:18:57.070 --> 00:19:07.110
Right. So this isn't quite a shmup by my standards because there's no power-ups and you don't progress throughout the screen.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:13.890
But there are some cool things that happen that are still, like, in the realm of design for millipede and centipede.

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:22.330
And that's those, like, gas explosions that you can hit throughout the stage to cause, like, a bunch of things to go away at once.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:23.930
Did you guys use those at all?

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.350
Was that just something that you got lucky and hit?

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:27.810
Was there a strategy to them?

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:29.510
I tried to hit them.

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.030
I was very unlucky.

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:35.550
I think once I was able to clear, like, a whole millipede with one, but it was by accident.

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:46.830
I think I tried at first until I realized that I was just more hurting myself by, like, focusing too much on, like, trying to get, like, some big explosion to take out as many enemies.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.690
And I was not focusing on things I should be.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:53.470
So then by the end, I would just hit them when I organically hit them, and it would either help or not.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:20:01.250
Yeah, I think this is one of the few instances of something that's, like, it's great that it's there because it also helps, like, beginner players by chance.

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:05.810
You know, like, it's just they're littered throughout the screen and there's a chance for you to hit them and that can help you.

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:12.610
And it's also, like, great for pro players who know what they're doing and can time those things to take out an entire millipede swarm.

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:17.930
You know, I think very rarely in these NES game designs are we seeing things that are helpful to both.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:28.490
A lot of times in NES games that we've been playing, it's either the game is hard and it's great for hardcore players or it's too easy because of extra things that were added into it.

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:39.630
Yeah, I guess it can kind of also be chalked up to a little bit of RNG because I imagine that this game is maybe not, like, in the classic sense of, like, procedurally generated.

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:44.390
But I don't see any pattern here in, like, where the mushrooms are.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:21:00.770
So maybe that's just because it's not a negative randomness, but a randomness that has the possibility of helping you that it can be helpful to both the casual and the more hardcore.

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:06.610
This might feel a little silly to ask, but what else should have been added to a game like this?

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:12.490
We kind of briefly complained about how much is going on on the screen, but are there things that we could have added?

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:15.430
Like, I'm thinking, you know, that stage clear thing would have been great.

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:20.570
But also, is there another way to do a different variety of stages here?

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:25.010
Obviously, this is more of a pipe dream thing, so don't let the limitations bother you here.

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:29.810
But what would you guys have liked to see to keep this game fresh as you continue to play it?

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:38.770
I'd say, like, maybe a more handcrafted level design so that it's not like a main game.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:40.630
Like, don't replace the main game with this.

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:55.010
But if there was, like, a challenge mode where it was preset, like, a preset design and there was a correct place to shoot to, like, get the whole level over in, like, 30 seconds or something like that.

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:57.450
I mean, that could be something fun.

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:04.510
But in terms of general game design here, I think there's already so much that adding anything more would be a problem.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:07.330
So that's my opinion on it.

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:09.310
Totally. Joe, anything to add?

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.030
I mean, I don't have anything off the top of my head.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:17.770
But I do really like that idea of, like, an alternate mode like that that just has, like, little interesting challenges that are just unique.

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:30.590
But, yeah, I feel like, you know, this game is targeting people who just, who like this really fast, like, how high of a score can I get experience.

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:41.510
So I don't know that it needs to, like, do anything else that's particularly special other than just, like, continue to get harder and harder and, you know, faster pace and keep it exciting, you know?

00:22:41.710 --> 00:22:50.930
Yeah, the easiest thing I could think of was just adding a two-player mode because there's really nothing, you know, limiting the idea of, like, you know, you would be able to work in tandem.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:52.870
It wouldn't be a competitive two-player.

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:55.350
I like, you know, but you would still be gaining points separately.

00:22:55.350 --> 00:23:03.330
I like that idea of just because there's so much going on on the screen, a friend helping you out might actually work in favor of the game because it would be less chaotic.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:08.030
You know, you take the left side, I'll take the right side, and then when they come down, it's like, okay, you stay up, I'll stay down.

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:10.710
Yeah, I think that that could be fun.

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:11.730
Definitely. Cool.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:15.190
All right, so, I mean, that's pretty much it from a gameplay perspective.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:22.170
But, you know, we have to talk about the fact that Millipede is actually a sequel to Centipede, which I think is brilliant.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.530
I think that's a great, like, where do we go from Centipede?

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.350
Well, add more legs.

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:30.630
But there's not much different between the two games.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:42.210
Really, the only difference is that the Millipede moves faster, and I think its head segment is more difficult to hit as a result of its moving faster.

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:47.210
So it was just considered a more difficult version of the classic Centipede game.

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:53.990
